const CourseLectures=[{"id":"circuit-basics","title":"Circuit models, references and power","subtitle":"L01 · Circuit basic theory","outcomes":["Replace components with ideal circuit models.","Keep voltage and current reference directions consistent.","Distinguish supplied power from absorbed power."],"topics":[{"id":"models","title":"From a physical circuit to a model","sources":[{"deck":"basic","page":10},{"deck":"basic","page":11}],"body":"A circuit connects a power source, a load, a switch and conducting wires. In the model, a battery becomes a voltage source and a lamp can be represented by a resistor. The model keeps the electrical behaviour needed for the calculation; it does not reproduce every physical detail.\nPassive elements include resistance, inductance and capacitance. Sources are active elements. Distinguish an ideal element from a real component: a real source has internal resistance, and a real coil can have both inductance and resistance."},{"id":"resistance","title":"Resistors, open circuits and short circuits","sources":[{"deck":"basic","page":12},{"deck":"basic","page":13},{"deck":"basic","page":14},{"deck":"basic","page":21}],"body":"For a linear resistor, the voltage–current characteristic is a straight line through the origin and its slope is constant. Resistance is measured in Ω; 1 kΩ = 1000 Ω and 1 MΩ = 10⁶ Ω. A nonlinear element does not have one constant resistance valid over its whole characteristic.\n$$u=Ri$$\nAn open circuit carries zero current; the surrounding circuit determines its voltage. A short circuit has zero voltage; the surrounding circuit determines its current. An open circuit can be represented by a zero-current ideal current source, and a short by a zero-voltage ideal voltage source.\nDo not infer that an open-circuit voltage or a short-circuit current must also be zero.","experiment":"ohm","warning":"The nonlinear-resistor graphic writes R ≠ U/I. A nonlinear element can still have a static resistance U/I at one operating point; what fails is a single constant R that describes the whole curve. Differential resistance du/di is a different quantity."},{"id":"sources","title":"Ideal and practical sources","sources":[{"deck":"basic","page":15},{"deck":"basic","page":16},{"deck":"basic","page":17},{"deck":"basic","page":18},{"deck":"basic","page":19},{"deck":"basic","page":20}],"body":"An ideal voltage source fixes its terminal voltage. The load determines its current. An ideal current source fixes its current; the external circuit determines its voltage. These ideal models have no built-in power limit.\nA practical voltage-source model places an internal resistance in series with an ideal voltage source. With current directed out of the positive terminal:\n$$U=U_S-IR_S$$\nA practical current-source model places an internal resistance in parallel with an ideal current source:\n$$I=I_S-\\frac{U}{R_S}$$\nThe voltage-source model approaches an ideal voltage source as its series resistance tends to zero. The current-source model approaches an ideal current source as its parallel resistance tends to infinity.\nA nonzero ideal voltage source may be open-circuited but cannot consistently be connected directly across an ideal short. A nonzero ideal current source may be short-circuited but cannot consistently drive an ideal open circuit.","check":{"question":"Which connection is consistent for a nonzero ideal current source?","options":["An ideal short circuit","An ideal open circuit"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A short sets voltage to zero while the source still fixes its current. A nonzero ideal source cannot satisfy an open circuit’s zero-current requirement."}},{"id":"references","title":"Choose directions before calculating","sources":[{"deck":"basic","page":22},{"deck":"basic","page":23},{"deck":"basic","page":24},{"deck":"basic","page":25},{"deck":"basic","page":26},{"deck":"basic","page":27}],"body":"Conventional current points in the direction of positive-charge motion. A voltage reference can be shown by + and − signs, an arrow from the marked positive terminal to the negative terminal, or ordered subscripts. Uab means Va − Vb; Iab is positive from a to b.\nChoose reference directions even when the physical direction is unknown. Solve the signed equations. A positive result agrees with your reference; a negative result points the other way. Do not change a reference halfway through a calculation.\nIf the current enters the resistor terminal marked +, u = Ri. If it enters the terminal marked −, u = −Ri. The resistor has not changed; the references have."},{"id":"dc-power","title":"Absorbing and supplying electrical power","sources":[{"deck":"basic","page":28},{"deck":"basic","page":29},{"deck":"basic","page":30}],"body":"With current entering the terminal marked positive, power absorbed by a two-terminal part is:\n$$p=ui$$\nPositive absorbed power means energy enters that part. Negative absorbed power means it supplies energy to the rest of the circuit. A source can absorb power as well as supply it: the signs must decide the result.\nIf the current reference leaves the positive terminal, absorbed power is −ui. Always state which convention you use. For a resistor, absorbed power is nonnegative:\n$$P=UI=I^2R=\\frac{U^2}{R}$$\nWhen checking a complete circuit, the algebraic sum of all absorbed powers is zero. Supplied power balances absorbed power."}]},{"id":"circuit-laws","title":"Kirchhoff’s laws and circuit equations","subtitle":"L01 · Laws, transformations and analysis","outcomes":["Write independent KCL and KVL equations.","Transform practical sources without changing terminal behaviour.","Use branch, loop and mesh methods, including controlled sources."],"topics":[{"id":"topology","title":"Nodes, branches, loops and trees","sources":[{"deck":"basic","page":31},{"deck":"basic","page":32},{"deck":"basic","page":33},{"deck":"original","page":38},{"deck":"original","page":39},{"deck":"original","page":40},{"deck":"original","page":41}],"body":"A node joins connected terminals at one potential. A branch is a path between nodes; be explicit whether you are counting individual element branches or an unbranched series path as one branch. A loop is a closed path. A mesh is a loop with no other loop inside it in a planar drawing.\nA tree connects every node without a loop. Its branches are tree branches; the remaining branches form the cotree and are called links. Adding one link to a tree creates a fundamental loop. Choose that loop’s direction to agree with its link current.\nFor a connected graph with n nodes and b branches:\n$$\\text{tree branches}=n-1,\\qquad\\text{links}=b-n+1$$\nThree branches between two nodes give three possible pairwise loops, but only two independent loops. A connected four-node, six-branch graph has three independent loops. A different drawing of the same connections does not change the circuit."},{"id":"kirchhoff-current","title":"Kirchhoff’s current law","sources":[{"deck":"basic","page":34},{"deck":"basic","page":35},{"deck":"basic","page":36}],"body":"Charge does not accumulate at an ideal junction. The current entering equals the current leaving, at every instant. Either choose entering currents positive or choose leaving currents positive, then use the choice consistently.\n$$\\sum_k i_k=0$$\nKCL also applies to a closed boundary enclosing part of a circuit. A two-terminal network has equal-and-opposite terminal currents; the algebraic sum of the three terminal currents of a three-terminal network is zero. A network with only one connection cannot carry a sustained net current through that connection.\nOnly n − 1 node equations are independent in a connected n-node circuit. The last equation follows by adding the others.","experiment":"kcl","check":{"question":"Around a connected pair of junctions, the entering current references are 7 A, 10 A and −12 A. The leaving currents are 4 A and i₁. What is i₁?","options":["−1 A","1 A","2 A","5 A"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Apply KCL to the boundary: 7 + 10 + (−12) = 4 + i₁, so i₁ = 1 A. The negative current already accounts for reversal of its entering reference."}},{"id":"kirchhoff-voltage","title":"Kirchhoff’s voltage law","sources":[{"deck":"basic","page":37},{"deck":"basic","page":38},{"deck":"basic","page":39}],"body":"Choose a direction around a closed loop. Add the signed voltage changes: a rise and a drop have opposite signs. Their algebraic sum is zero.\n$$\\sum_k u_k=0$$\nFor a single loop with opposing sources E₁ and E₂, the source and resistor references give R₃I + E₁ + R₁I + R₂I − E₂ = 0. In the second example, R₁ = 1 Ω, R₂ = 2 Ω, R₃ = 3 Ω, E₃ = 3 V and I₃ = 3 A. The calculation gives I₂ = 6 A, I₁ = 9 A and E₁ = 21 V; E₁ supplies 189 W, E₃ absorbs 9 W, and the resistors absorb 81 + 72 + 27 = 180 W. The absorbed powers sum to −189 + 9 + 180 = 0 W.\nTogether, n − 1 independent KCL equations and b − n + 1 independent KVL equations supply b equations for branch analysis.","warning":"The KVL wording in the lecture says “the algebraic sum of the current or voltage is 0.” KVL sums voltages around a loop. KCL sums currents at a node; loop currents do not generally sum to zero.","check":{"question":"For a connected graph with n nodes and b branches, how many independent KVL equations are needed in branch analysis?","options":["n − 1","b − n + 1","b + n − 1"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A tree has n − 1 branches, leaving b − n + 1 links and independent fundamental loops."}},{"id":"source-transformation","title":"Equivalent source transformations","sources":[{"deck":"basic","page":40},{"deck":"basic","page":41},{"deck":"basic","page":42},{"deck":"basic","page":43},{"deck":"basic","page":44},{"deck":"basic","page":45},{"deck":"basic","page":46},{"deck":"basic","page":47},{"deck":"basic","page":48},{"deck":"basic","page":49},{"deck":"basic","page":50}],"body":"Two networks are externally equivalent when every permitted load sees the same terminal voltage and current. Internal currents and powers need not match.\nA voltage source Uₛ in series with Rₛ is equivalent to a current source Iₛ in parallel with the same resistance when:\n$$I_S=\\frac{U_S}{R_S},\\qquad U_S=I_SR_S$$\nChoose source polarities so the same terminal is positive under open-circuit conditions. Do not transform an ideal source by inventing a finite internal resistance.\nSeries voltage sources combine algebraically; parallel current sources combine algebraically. Preserve directions when sources oppose one another. An ideal voltage source fixes the voltage of a parallel branch, while an ideal current source fixes the current of a series branch; simplifications preserve external behaviour, not the removed elements’ internal power.\nA 10 V source in series with 2 Ω becomes a 5 A source in parallel with 2 Ω. The current-source arrow points toward the terminal that the voltage source makes positive.","sections":[{"title":"Successive transformations and power","body":"Let A and B be two upper nodes with common ground. At A, connect three branches to ground: R₁ = 2 Ω in series with a 12 V source (positive above ground), R₂ = 4 Ω, and R₃ = 4 Ω in series with a 16 V source (positive above ground). Connect R₅ = 5 Ω from A to B. At B, connect R₄ = 4 Ω to ground and a 3 A current source directed upward from ground. Define I through R₅ from A to B.\nTransform the two left voltage-source branches: 12/2 = 6 A and 16/4 = 4 A inject into A. Their parallel resistance with R₂ is 1/(1/2 + 1/4 + 1/4) = 1 Ω. This becomes a 10 V source with 1 Ω series resistance. The right source becomes 12 V with 4 Ω series resistance.\n$$I=\\frac{10-12}{1+5+4}=-0.2\\ \\mathrm A$$\nThe actual current through R₅ flows from B toward A. R₄ carries 3 − 0.2 = 2.8 A downward, so Vᵦ = 11.2 V and Vₐ = 10.2 V. The right-hand two-terminal part absorbs VᵦI = −2.24 W and therefore supplies 2.24 W. Inside it, the current source absorbs −3(11.2) = −33.6 W and R₄ absorbs 31.36 W. Internal powers change under source transformation even though terminal power is preserved."}]},{"id":"branch-analysis","title":"Branch-current equations","sources":[{"deck":"basic","page":51},{"deck":"basic","page":52},{"deck":"basic","page":53},{"deck":"basic","page":54},{"deck":"basic","page":55},{"deck":"basic","page":56},{"deck":"original","page":42},{"deck":"original","page":43},{"deck":"original","page":44},{"deck":"original","page":45}],"body":"Assign a reference current to every unknown branch. Write independent node equations, then independent loop equations, substituting each element’s voltage–current relation. Solve the simultaneous equations and interpret negative answers against the original arrows.\nAn ideal current-source branch has a known current but an unknown source voltage. Include that voltage when a loop equation requires it, or choose equations that eliminate it. Do not apply Ohm’s law across the ideal source.\nA controlled source remains linked to its controlling voltage or current. For a voltage-controlled current source, i = gₘu, where gₘ is in siemens. Add its control equation to the circuit equations. The control relation must be solved together with KCL and KVL, including when its result is negative.","sections":[{"title":"Writing a complete branch system","body":"For two source branches feeding a shared resistor, define I₁ and I₂ toward the top junction and I₃ down R₃. Then I₁ + I₂ = I₃, R₁I₁ + R₃I₃ = U₁ and R₂I₂ + R₃I₃ = U₂. These are one independent node equation and two independent loop equations.\nFor the four-node bridge, name the vertices a (left), b (top), c (right), d (bottom). The branches are a→b through R₁ with I₁, b→c through R₂ with I₂, c→a through R₃ and U₃ with I₃, a→d through R₄ and U₄ with I₄, d→c through R₅ with I₅, and b→d through R₆ with I₆. U₃ and U₄ have their positive terminals toward a.\n$$I_3=I_1+I_4,\\quad I_1=I_2+I_6,\\quad I_2+I_5=I_3,\\quad I_4+I_6=I_5$$\nChoose any three independent node equations. Three independent loop equations are:\n$$R_1I_1+R_6I_6=R_4I_4+U_4$$\n$$R_2I_2+R_5I_5-R_6I_6=0$$\n$$R_4I_4+R_5I_5+R_3I_3+U_4-U_3=0$$\nA bridge with a known current-source branch needs only five unknown branch currents. Choose loops avoiding that source to eliminate its unknown voltage; an additional loop through the source determines its voltage afterward rather than adding an independent constraint on the five currents."},{"title":"Independent- and controlled-source examples","body":"Independent-source example: between a and ground, put a 2 V source with its negative terminal at a in series with R₁ = 1 Ω; put R₃ = 2 Ω in a second branch; and put a 0.5 A source directed upward in a third branch. Define I₁ and I₃ downward through the two passive branches. Then I₁ + I₃ = 0.5 and 2 = I₁ − 2I₃. Thus I₁ = 1 A, I₃ = −0.5 A and Vₐ = −1 V. The voltage source supplies 2 W, the current source absorbs 0.5 W, and the resistors absorb 1 + 0.5 = 1.5 W.\nControlled-source example: connect R₂ = 2 Ω between a and ground, and a branch containing R₃ = 3 Ω followed by a 3 V source whose positive terminal is grounded. A controlled current source injects gₘU₃ into a, where U₃ is the drop across R₃ from a toward the source, and gₘ = 1 S. Set I₂ downward in R₂ and I₃ from a through R₃. Then U₃ = 3I₃, gₘU₃ = I₂ + I₃, and 3 = −2I₂ + 3I₃. Hence I₂ = −6 A, I₃ = −3 A and Vₐ = −12 V."}],"warning":"In the independent-source example of the original Circuit Basic Theory deck, the current-source power is written as −0.5 W and labelled absorbed. With the arrow entering its lower terminal and Vab = −1 V, its absorbed power is −Vab·Is = +0.5 W. The circuit power balance confirms absorption."},{"id":"loop-mesh","title":"Loop currents, mesh currents and supermeshes","sources":[{"deck":"original","page":46},{"deck":"original","page":47},{"deck":"original","page":48},{"deck":"original","page":49},{"deck":"original","page":50},{"deck":"original","page":51},{"deck":"original","page":52},{"deck":"original","page":53},{"deck":"original","page":54}],"body":"Loop analysis uses independent circulating currents. Choose a tree, assign a reference current to each link, and write KVL for each fundamental loop. Express shared branch currents as signed sums of loop currents.\nThe diagonal resistance coefficient is the sum of resistances in a loop. An off-diagonal coefficient comes from shared resistances, with its sign determined by the relative loop-current directions through those elements. Source terms are signed voltage rises in the chosen loop direction.\nFor a planar circuit, mesh currents offer a convenient independent-loop choice. With all mesh currents clockwise, a shared resistor contributes R(I₁ − I₂) to mesh 1. An ideal current source on a shared boundary gives a current constraint; write KVL around the surrounding supermesh and retain that constraint.\nKeep dependent-source control relations in the equation system. A current source on an outer mesh boundary directly fixes that mesh current. A current source between two meshes fixes their difference; it does not remove the need for the supermesh equation.","sections":[{"title":"Matrix form and worked constraints","body":"For two clockwise meshes sharing R₃, the resistor-only matrix is:\n$$\\begin{bmatrix}R_1+R_3&-R_3\\\\-R_3&R_2+R_3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}I_{m1}\\\\I_{m2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}U_1\\\\-U_2\\end{bmatrix}$$\nThe minus sign on U₂ follows the right source’s top-positive reference: the clockwise right mesh crosses it from + to −. In a general loop basis, shared-resistance signs depend on the chosen directions; they are not automatically negative.\nIn the fixed-current example, R₁ = 1 Ω, R₄ = 4 Ω, R₅ = 5 Ω, R₆ = 6 Ω; two selected loop currents are fixed by Iₛ₂ = 2 A and Iₛ₃ = 3 A. The remaining loop equation is (R₁ + R₄ + R₆)I₁ + R₄I₂ − R₆I₃ = Uₛ₄, with Uₛ₄ = 4 V. Thus I₁ = 14/11 A. Using the branch relations I₄ = I₁ + I₂, I₅ = I₂ + I₃, I₆ = I₁ − I₃ gives I₄ = 36/11 A, I₅ = 5 A and I₆ = −19/11 A.\nFor the controlled-source example with R₁ = 1 Ω, R₂ = 2 Ω, R₃ = 3 Ω, R₄ = 4 Ω and Iₛ₅ = 5 A, the chosen loop equations give I₄ = 25/(10 + 12gₘ), and the requested zero branch current is I₃ = I₄ − 5 = 0. Therefore gₘ = −5/12 S. A negative gain is a valid signed result for the chosen control references.\nFor the three-mesh example, E₁ = 1 V, E₃ = 6 V, Iₛ = 6 A, R₁ = 3 Ω, R₂ = 2 Ω, R₃ = 1 Ω and R₄ = 4 Ω. The equations are Iₘ₃ = 6 A, 5Iₘ₁ − 2Iₘ₂ = 1 and −2Iₘ₁ + 7Iₘ₂ = 6 + 1(6). They give Iₘ₁ = 1 A and Iₘ₂ = 2 A.\nThe controlled-voltage example uses Rₛ = R₁ = 1 Ω, R₂ = 2 Ω, R₃ = 3 Ω and μ = 3. With U = Uₛ₁ − RₛIₘ₁, write (Rₛ + R₁ + R₂)Iₘ₁ − R₂Iₘ₂ = Uₛ₁ − μU and −R₂Iₘ₁ + (R₂ + R₃)Iₘ₂ = μU. Elimination gives 7Iₘ₂ = 5Uₛ₁. Requiring I₃ = Iₘ₂ = 3 A gives Uₛ₁ = 4.2 V."}]}]},{"id":"dc-methods","title":"Node voltages and equivalent networks","subtitle":"L02–L03 · DC circuit methods","outcomes":["Choose ground and solve node equations.","Apply superposition, Thévenin and Norton correctly.","Find equivalent resistance and maximum load power."],"topics":[{"id":"potential","title":"Potential depends on the reference","sources":[{"deck":"dc","page":3},{"deck":"dc","page":4},{"deck":"dc","page":5}],"body":"Choose one node as the reference and call its potential zero. Every other node potential is its voltage relative to that reference. Changing ground shifts node potentials but does not change the voltage between two physical nodes.\n$$U_{ab}=V_a-V_b$$\nA supply labelled +12 V or −6 V specifies a potential relative to the common reference, not an extra series element. Redraw the circuit with a common ground if the shorthand is confusing."},{"id":"nodal","title":"The node-voltage method","sources":[{"deck":"dc","page":6},{"deck":"dc","page":7},{"deck":"dc","page":8},{"deck":"dc","page":9},{"deck":"dc","page":10},{"deck":"dc","page":11},{"deck":"dc","page":12},{"deck":"original","page":55},{"deck":"original","page":56},{"deck":"original","page":57},{"deck":"original","page":58},{"deck":"original","page":59},{"deck":"original","page":60}],"body":"Choose ground, label the other node voltages, express branch currents using those voltages and write KCL. For a resistor from node a to node b, current from a to b is (Va − Vb)/R. Include any series source voltage with its marked sign.\nFor the resistor part of a conductance matrix, a diagonal coefficient sums the conductances connected to that node. An off-diagonal coefficient is minus the conductance directly joining the two nodes. The source vector contains the signed source contributions. Controlled sources add their own relationships; they cannot simply be removed.\nFor a branch containing several series resistors, use its equivalent conductance. For example, G₆ = 1/(R₆′ + R₆″) = G₆′G₆″/(G₆′ + G₆″), not G₆′ + G₆″. A series ideal current source fixes its branch current, so its series resistor does not add an independent nodal-conductance term.\nNode analysis is especially economical when there are many branches and few nodes. Choose the method with the smallest useful set of unknowns for the quantity being requested.","sections":[{"title":"One node and two nodes","body":"A one-node network with resistor branches to fixed potentials U₁, U₂, 0 and −U₄, plus sources Iₛ₁ entering and Iₛ₂ leaving, gives:\n$$V_A=\\frac{U_1/R_1+U_2/R_2-U_4/R_4+I_{S1}-I_{S2}}{1/R_1+1/R_2+1/R_3+1/R_4}$$\nA resistor in series with an ideal current source does not appear in this denominator. The ideal source still sets the branch current.\nFor two unknown nodes A and B: connect A to U₁ through R₁, to U₂ through R₂, and to B through R₃. Connect B to ground through R₄ and to −U₅ through R₅. Then:\n$$(1/R_1+1/R_2+1/R_3)V_A-V_B/R_3=U_1/R_1+U_2/R_2$$\n$$-V_A/R_3+(1/R_3+1/R_4+1/R_5)V_B=-U_5/R_5$$\nFor the numerical example, R₁ = R₁′ = 0.5 Ω, R₃ = R₄ = R₅ = R₆ = 1 Ω, Uₛ₁ = 1 V, Uₛ₃ = 3 V, Iₛ₂ = 2 A and Iₛ₆ = 6 A. The node equations reduce to 3V₁ − 2V₂ = −4 and −2V₁ + 3V₂ = 9. Therefore V₁ = 1.2 V and V₂ = 3.8 V. The corresponding currents are I₁ = (1 − V₁)/1 = −0.2 A, I₃ = (V₁ − V₂ + 3)/1 = 0.4 A, I₄ = (V₁ − V₂)/1 = −2.6 A and I₅ = V₂/1 = 3.8 A.\nHere the two nodes are joined by R₄ and, in parallel, R₃ in series with a 3 V source positive toward node 2. Node 1 connects through R₁′, the 1 V source (positive toward R₁′), and R₁ to ground; a 2 A source leaves node 1. Node 2 connects through R₅ to ground and receives 6 A from a source in series with R₆. The resistance R₆ does not affect that injected current."},{"title":"Controlled sources remain in the node equations","body":"In the controlled-source example, choose d as ground. The 10 V source fixes Vc = 10 V. R₁ = R₂ = R₃ = R₄ = R₆ = 2 Ω, R₇ = 6 Ω, Uₛ₁ = 4 V, Iₛ₇ = 1 A and Iₛ₈ = 4 A. The gains are μ = 2 S and β = 2. The controlling voltage is U = Vb − Va + 4 and the controlling current is I = (Vb − Vc)/2.\nR₁ and the 4 V source form the a–b branch (source positive at a); R₂ connects b–c, R₃ and R₄ connect c–a, and R₆ connects a–d. The controlled sources μU and βI point a→b and a→c. The 4 A source points d→b; the 1 A source and its series R₇ connect d→a.\n$$2V_a-\\tfrac12V_b-V_c=2-2U-2I+1$$\n$$-\\tfrac12V_a+V_b-\\tfrac12V_c=2U-2+4$$\nSubstitution gives Vb = 6 V and Va = 14 V, so U = −4 V and I = −2 A in this particular solution. Do not delete the controlled sources before solving: their signed values follow from the complete equations."}],"warning":"The two-node equation on page 9 of L02+L03 prints the wrong self-conductances for node B. The connected branches are R₃, R₄ and R₅, so the diagonal term must be 1/R₃ + 1/R₄ + 1/R₅, as corrected on the following page. Use the connections to form the equation. The controlled-source example on page 60 of the original Circuit Basic Theory deck also lists Vb = 10 V. With its drawn references and components, KCL gives Vb = 6 V and Va = 14 V, as derived here."},{"id":"supernodes","title":"Voltage sources between unknown nodes","sources":[{"deck":"original","page":61},{"deck":"original","page":62},{"deck":"original","page":63}],"body":"An ideal voltage source between two nonreference nodes fixes their difference but does not give its own current through a resistance formula.\nModified nodal analysis introduces the source current as another unknown and adds the voltage constraint. Alternatively, surround the two nodes and the source with a supernode: write KCL for currents crossing its boundary, then add the same voltage constraint.\n$$V_b-V_c=U_{S2}$$\nIf one source terminal is grounded, the other node voltage is already fixed by the source polarity. Use that value directly.","sections":[{"title":"Supernode equations","body":"For the drawn bridge, let Vₐ = Uₛ₁ and the right rail be ground. R₃ connects a to b, R₄ connects a to c, R₅ connects b to ground, R₆ connects c to ground, and Uₛ₂ is positive at b relative to c. Add the KCL equations for b and c:\n$$\\frac{V_a-V_b}{R_3}+\\frac{V_a-V_c}{R_4}=\\frac{V_b}{R_5}+\\frac{V_c}{R_6}$$\nSolve this together with Vᵦ − V꜀ = Uₛ₂. If using modified nodal analysis instead, introduce the source current directed b→c; it enters the b and c KCL equations with opposite signs and cancels when those equations are added."}]},{"id":"superposition","title":"Superposition and linearity","sources":[{"deck":"dc","page":13},{"deck":"dc","page":14},{"deck":"dc","page":15},{"deck":"dc","page":16},{"deck":"dc","page":17},{"deck":"dc","page":18}],"body":"In a linear circuit, a voltage or current response equals the algebraic sum of the responses to the independent sources acting separately. To deactivate an independent ideal voltage source, set it to zero volts: short it. To deactivate an independent ideal current source, set it to zero amperes: open it.\nKeep resistances, connections and reference directions unchanged. Dependent sources remain active because their controlling relationships are part of the circuit.\nYou can group independent sources instead of considering just one at a time. Add signed currents or voltages. Do not add the separately calculated powers: squaring the total current introduces cross terms.\n$$R(I_1+I_2)^2\\ne RI_1^2+RI_2^2\\quad\\text{in general}$$\nIn the 10 V / 10 A example, the 3 Ω resistor connects the 10 V source to the top node, while 2 Ω connects that node to ground. The 10 A source injects upward. Define I₁ from the 10 V source toward the node. With only the voltage source, I₁′ = 10/(3 + 2) = 2 A. With only the current source, I₁″ = −10·2/(3 + 2) = −4 A. Thus I₁ = −2 A.\nFor a node connected to +12 V through 3 Ω and to −6 V through 6 Ω, Vₐ = 12·6/(3 + 6) − 6·3/(3 + 6) = 6 V. The separate contributions are +8 V and −2 V.","check":{"question":"Which quantity can you add directly across the separate source calculations?","options":["Signed branch currents","The separately calculated resistor powers"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Superposition applies to linear voltages and currents. Resistor power depends on the square of the total current."}},{"id":"thevenin-norton","title":"Thévenin and Norton equivalents","sources":[{"deck":"dc","page":19},{"deck":"dc","page":20},{"deck":"dc","page":21},{"deck":"dc","page":22},{"deck":"dc","page":23},{"deck":"dc","page":24},{"deck":"dc","page":25},{"deck":"dc","page":26},{"deck":"dc","page":27},{"deck":"dc","page":28},{"deck":"dc","page":29},{"deck":"dc","page":30},{"deck":"dc","page":32}],"body":"A two-terminal network connects to its load through one port. A linear network with a finite Thévenin equivalent can be represented by its open-circuit voltage in series with its equivalent resistance. A finite Norton equivalent uses the short-circuit current in parallel with the equivalent resistance. An ideal voltage source has zero series resistance and no finite Norton current; an ideal current source has infinite parallel resistance and no finite Thévenin voltage.\n$$U_{\\rm th}=U_{\\rm oc},\\qquad I_N=I_{\\rm sc},\\qquad R_N=R_{\\rm th}$$\nRemove the load before finding the equivalent. With independent sources deactivated, calculate the resistance looking into the port. If dependent sources exist, retain them and use a test source as needed. Reconnect the load only after finding the equivalent.\n$$I_L=\\frac{U_{\\rm th}}{R_{\\rm th}+R_L},\\qquad U_L=\\frac{U_{\\rm th}R_L}{R_{\\rm th}+R_L}$$\nThe bridge example has a 15 V supply, R₁ = 20 Ω, R₂ = 30 Ω, R₃ = 30 Ω, R₄ = 20 Ω and R₅ = 6 Ω. The port reduces to 3 V in series with 24 Ω, giving I₅ = 0.1 A in the marked direction. The second example reduces to 9 V and 57 Ω; with a 33 Ω load, the marked load voltage is 3.3 V.","experiment":"thevenin","sections":[{"title":"Work through the port reductions","body":"In the bridge, the source connects the left rail at +15 V to the right rail at 0 V. The upper midpoint A is between 20 Ω and 30 Ω; the lower midpoint B is between 30 Ω and 20 Ω. With the 6 Ω load removed, Vₐ = 15·30/50 = 9 V and Vᵦ = 15·20/50 = 6 V. Thus Uoc = Vₐ − Vᵦ = 3 V. Shorting the independent voltage source gives Rth = (20∥30) + (30∥20) = 24 Ω. Reconnecting 6 Ω yields I₅ = 3/(24 + 6) = 0.1 A from A to B.\nSecond example: the top path from D to A contains 50 Ω, then a 10 V source with its positive terminal at A. Between D and E are a 4 Ω resistor and, in parallel, another 4 Ω resistor in series with an 8 V source positive toward D. Between E and B are a 5 Ω resistor and, in parallel, a 1 A source directed E→B. The load is 33 Ω from A to B, with UL = Vₐ − Vᵦ.\nWith the load removed, Uoc = 10 + 8·4/(4 + 4) − 1·5 = 9 V. Deactivating the sources gives Rth = 50 + (4∥4) + 5 = 57 Ω. Consequently UL = 9·33/(57 + 33) = 3.3 V and IL = 0.1 A."}]},{"id":"maximum-power","title":"Maximum power into a resistive load","sources":[{"deck":"dc","page":31}],"body":"For a fixed Thévenin source with positive internal resistance, the load power depends on the load resistance:\n$$P_L=\\frac{U_{\\rm th}^2R_L}{(R_{\\rm th}+R_L)^2}$$\nDifferentiating with respect to the load resistance gives maximum power at resistance matching:\n$$R_L=R_{\\rm th},\\qquad P_{\\max}=\\frac{U_{\\rm th}^2}{4R_{\\rm th}}$$\nAt matching, the load voltage is Uth/2. In the Thévenin source model, equal power is dissipated in the internal resistance and the load, so its efficiency is 50%. This does not establish the total efficiency of the original network: a terminal equivalent does not preserve all internal powers.","sections":[{"title":"Differentiate the load-power expression","body":"$$\\frac{dP_L}{dR_L}=U_{\\rm th}^2\\frac{R_{\\rm th}-R_L}{(R_{\\rm th}+R_L)^3}$$\nFor positive Rth, the derivative is positive below matching and negative above it. The maximum is therefore global over positive load resistances. This resistive result is distinct from the sinusoidal AC conjugate-match condition ZL = Zth* when both the load resistance and reactance can be chosen."}]},{"id":"star-delta","title":"Star–delta resistance transformations","sources":[{"deck":"dc","page":33},{"deck":"dc","page":34},{"deck":"dc","page":35},{"deck":"dc","page":36},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":6}],"body":"A three-terminal star and delta are equivalent when every pair of terminals has the same resistance with the third terminal left open. For a delta with sides RAB, RBC and RCA, let Σ = RAB + RBC + RCA.\n$$r_A=\\frac{R_{AB}R_{CA}}{\\Sigma},\\quad r_B=\\frac{R_{AB}R_{BC}}{\\Sigma},\\quad r_C=\\frac{R_{BC}R_{CA}}{\\Sigma}$$\nConversely, with D = rArB + rBrC + rCrA:\n$$R_{AB}=\\frac{D}{r_C},\\quad R_{BC}=\\frac{D}{r_A},\\quad R_{CA}=\\frac{D}{r_B}$$\nFor three equal resistors, a delta side R corresponds to a star arm r = R/3. Use this transformation when a bridge cannot be reduced by simple series and parallel combinations.","sections":[{"title":"Reduce a resistor bridge","body":"Suppose the bridge has R₁ from A to B, R₂ from A to C, R₀ from B to C, R₃ from B to D, and R₄ from C to D. The triangle A–B–C cannot be reduced directly as series/parallel resistors. Replace it by star arms rA, rB and rC. The remaining resistance between A and D is:\n$$R_{AD}=r_A+\\frac{(r_B+R_3)(r_C+R_4)}{r_B+R_3+r_C+R_4}$$\nThe conversion equations come from equating each open-third-terminal resistance, for example rA + rB = RAB∥(RCA + RBC), then solving the three equations together."}]},{"id":"equivalent-resistance","title":"Measuring and calculating equivalent resistance","sources":[{"deck":"ac-basics","page":1},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":2},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":3},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":4},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":5},{"deck":"dc","page":37},{"deck":"dc","page":38}],"body":"Simple source-deactivated networks can be reduced by series/parallel combinations. More general equivalents can be found from a suitable terminal test.\nOpen-circuit / short-circuit method, when the values are finite and Isc is nonzero:\n$$R_{\\rm th}=\\frac{U_{\\rm oc}}{I_{\\rm sc}}$$\nLoad method: measure open-circuit voltage and the voltage across a known load.\n$$R_{\\rm th}=R_L\\left(\\frac{U_{\\rm oc}}{U_L}-1\\right)$$\nTest-source method: deactivate independent sources, retain dependent sources and apply a test voltage. Then Rth = Utest/Itest, with current entering the positive test-voltage terminal.\nA finite-resistance voltmeter loads the circuit. The readings are 30 V with a 50 kΩ meter and 50 V with a 100 kΩ meter. In kΩ, solve 30 = Us·50/(Rs + 50) and 50 = Us·100/(Rs + 100), obtaining Us = 150 V and Rs = 200 kΩ. A 1 MΩ meter then reads 150·1000/(200 + 1000) = 125 V. The true unloaded voltage is 150 V; an ideal infinite-resistance meter is required to read it exactly in this model.\nDo not use Uoc/Isc when both are zero, as can occur in a network with only dependent sources. The test-source method remains available."},{"id":"homogeneity","title":"Scaling, unit-current reasoning and method selection","sources":[{"deck":"dc","page":39},{"deck":"dc","page":40},{"deck":"dc","page":41},{"deck":"dc","page":42},{"deck":"dc","page":43}],"body":"With one independent source in a linear circuit, scaling that source by a factor scales all branch voltages and currents by the same factor. Resistances and controlled-source gains stay unchanged.\nThe two-source example gives Uo = 8 V for Us = 4 V, Is = 2 A, and Uo = −2 V for Us = 2 V, Is = 4 A. Write Uo = aUs + bIs. Solving gives a = 3 and b = −2, so Us = 3 V and Is = 3 A give Uo = 3 V.\nThe unit-current method assumes a convenient output current, works backward through a resistor ladder to find the source voltage needed, and scales to the actual source. In the 51 V ladder, three 1 Ω series resistors connect successive nodes along the upper rail. Each node has a 1 Ω shunt to the common bottom rail; the last node also has the 2 Ω output resistor. A trial 1 A in that 2 Ω resistor gives 2 V at the last node. Its 1 Ω shunt carries 2 A, so the preceding series branch carries 3 A. Working left gives node voltages 5 V and 13 V, series currents 8 A and 21 A, and a trial supply of 34 V. Scale by 51/34: the requested output current is 1.5 A.\nFor a capacitor in a DC steady-state network, its branch is open. Find the potentials at its two terminals using the remaining circuit and subtract them in the marked voltage direction.","sections":[{"title":"DC capacitor example","body":"Terminal A connects to +12 V through 2 kΩ, ground through 3 kΩ, and −12 V through 6 kΩ. Terminal B connects to +6 V through 2 kΩ, ground through 4 kΩ, and −8 V through 4 kΩ. In DC steady state the capacitor between A and B is open, so:\n$$V_A=\\frac{12/2-12/6}{1/2+1/3+1/6}=4\\ \\mathrm V,\\qquad V_B=\\frac{6/2-8/4}{1/2+1/4+1/4}=1\\ \\mathrm V$$\nThus UC = Vₐ − Vᵦ = 3 V. The resistance unit cancels consistently because every resistance in each expression is in kΩ."}]}]},{"id":"sinusoidal-ac","title":"Sinusoids, phasors and R, L, C","subtitle":"L04–L05 / Week03 · Sinusoidal AC","outcomes":["Convert between waveforms, RMS values and phasors.","Calculate with complex numbers and consistent phase references.","Derive voltage, current and power for R, L and C."],"topics":[{"id":"sinusoids","title":"Amplitude, frequency and phase","sources":[{"deck":"ac-basics","page":8},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":9},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":10},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":11},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":12},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":13},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":14},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":15},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":16}],"body":"A periodic quantity repeats after a period T. A sinusoid is specified by amplitude, angular frequency and initial phase:\n$$i(t)=I_m\\sin(\\omega t+\\varphi),\\qquad f=\\frac1T,\\qquad\\omega=2\\pi f$$\nAmplitude is the peak value; frequency is in hertz; angular frequency is in radians per second. Initial phase is the phase at t = 0. Two sinusoids can be compared by a constant phase difference only when their frequencies are equal.\nA positive relative phase leads; a negative relative phase lags. Equal phases are in phase, and a difference of 180° means opposition. Adding same-frequency sinusoids produces a sinusoid at that frequency, or zero if the terms cancel exactly.\nRMS is the DC value that produces the same average heating in a resistor. For a sine wave only:\n$$I=\\frac{I_m}{\\sqrt2},\\qquad U=\\frac{U_m}{\\sqrt2}$$\nKeep instantaneous values i, u; RMS values I, U; and peak values Im, Um separate.","experiment":"waves","sections":[{"title":"RMS definition and numerical example","body":"$$I=\\sqrt{\\frac1T\\int_0^T i^2(t)\\,dt}$$\nFor i(t) = sin(1000t + 30°) A, Im = 1 A, I = 1/√2 ≈ 0.7071 A, ω = 1000 rad/s, f = 1000/(2π) ≈ 159.155 Hz, T ≈ 6.283 ms and the initial phase is 30°. Angular frequency and ordinary frequency are not interchangeable."}]},{"id":"phasor-representation","title":"Four ways to represent the same sinusoid","sources":[{"deck":"ac-basics","page":17},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":18},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":19},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":20},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":21},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":22},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":23},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":24},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":29},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":30},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":31},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":32}],"body":"A rotating segment of peak length projects onto the instantaneous sine wave. For an RMS-length segment, multiply its projection by √2 to recover the instantaneous value. Its initial angle represents phase. The course shows both peak phasors and RMS phasors; identify which convention is in use before using the magnitude.\nWith RMS phasors and a sine reference:\n$$u(t)=\\sqrt2U\\sin(\\omega t+\\varphi)\\quad\\longleftrightarrow\\quad\\underline U=U\\angle\\varphi$$\nA waveform, an instantaneous expression, a phasor diagram and a complex expression all describe the same single-frequency sinusoid. A phasor is not an instantaneous voltage or current; the shared time factor is omitted for calculation.\nAdd same-frequency phasors with the parallelogram rule. Do not put different frequencies into one phasor sum. The same-frequency requirement also means that ω is shared when converting a collection of phasors back to time functions.","experiment":"phasors","sections":[{"title":"Conversions in both directions","body":"For i(t) = 141.4 sin(314t + π/6) A and u(t) = 311.1 sin(314t − π/3) V, the RMS phasors are approximately:\n$$\\underline I=100\\angle30^\\circ=86.60+j50\\ \\mathrm A$$\n$$\\underline U=220\\angle(-60^\\circ)=110-j190.53\\ \\mathrm V$$\nFor f = 1000 Hz, I̲₁ = 100∠−60° A and I̲₂ = 10∠30° A give i₁(t) = 100√2 sin(2000πt − 60°) A and i₂(t) = 10√2 sin(2000πt + 30°) A. Using 6280 rad/s is the lecture’s rounded form of 2000π ≈ 6283.185 rad/s."}]},{"id":"complex-arithmetic","title":"Rectangular, polar and exponential forms","sources":[{"deck":"ac-basics","page":25},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":26},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":27},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":28},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":33},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":34},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":35},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":36},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":37}],"body":"On the complex plane, the horizontal axis is real and the vertical axis is imaginary. The symbol j satisfies j² = −1.\n$$\\underline U=a+jb=U(\\cos\\varphi+j\\sin\\varphi)=Ue^{j\\varphi}=U\\angle\\varphi$$\n$$U=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2},\\qquad\\varphi=\\operatorname{atan2}(b,a)$$\nChoose the correct quadrant; arctan(b/a) alone can lose 180°. Add and subtract rectangular components. Multiply polar magnitudes and add angles; divide magnitudes and subtract angles.\n$$A\\angle\\alpha\\;B\\angle\\beta=AB\\angle(\\alpha+\\beta),\\qquad\\frac{A\\angle\\alpha}{B\\angle\\beta}=\\frac AB\\angle(\\alpha-\\beta)$$\nDo not equate a phasor to an instantaneous function: they correspond under a declared frequency and convention, but they are different mathematical objects.","sections":[{"title":"Judge the notation","body":"The equality u = 100 sin ωt = U̲ is invalid: a time-varying real voltage is not the same object as its phasor. Likewise U̲ = 50e^(j15°) corresponds to u = 50√2 sin(ωt + 15°), but they should not be joined by an ordinary equality sign.\nIf I̲ = 100∠50° A, then i = 100√2 sin(ωt + 50°) A; omitting √2 confuses RMS with peak. For i = 10 sin(ωt + 45°) A, the RMS magnitude is I = 10/√2 A, without an angle; the peak phasor is I̲m = 10∠45° A. For u = 10√2 sin(ωt − 15°) V, U = 10 V is correct, while U̲ = 10∠+15° V has the wrong phase sign."}]},{"id":"resistor-ac","title":"A resistor under sinusoidal excitation","sources":[{"deck":"ac-basics","page":38},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":39},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":40},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":41},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":42},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":43},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":44},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":45},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":46},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":47},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":48},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":49}],"body":"Replace each time-domain variable with its phasor while preserving circuit connections and reference directions. For a resistor, u(t) = Ri(t), so voltage and current have equal frequency and phase.\n$$\\underline U=R\\underline I,\\qquad U=RI$$\nTaking i(t) = √2 I sin(ωt), instantaneous power is nonnegative and varies at twice the signal frequency:\n$$p(t)=2UI\\sin^2(\\omega t)=UI[1-\\cos(2\\omega t)]$$\n$$P=UI=I^2R=\\frac{U^2}{R},\\qquad Q=0$$\nFor R = 1 kΩ and u(t) = 100 sin(1000t) V, U = 100/√2 V, I = 100/√2 mA, i(t) = 100 sin(1000t) mA and P = U²/R = 5 W.\nThe RMS equation I = U/R and instantaneous equation i = u/R are both valid. The mixed equation i = U/R is invalid because a time-varying current is being equated to a constant RMS magnitude."},{"id":"inductor-ac","title":"An inductor: current lags voltage","sources":[{"deck":"ac-basics","page":50},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":51},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":52},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":53},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":54},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":55},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":56},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":57},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":58},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":59},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":60}],"body":"An inductor stores energy in its magnetic field. Its voltage depends on the rate of change of current:\n$$u=L\\frac{di}{dt},\\qquad X_L=\\omega L,\\qquad Z_L=j\\omega L$$\nWith sinusoidal excitation, voltage leads current by 90°. The RMS relation is U = XL I; the phasor relation also carries the phase:\n$$\\underline U=jX_L\\underline I$$\nHigher frequency or greater inductance produces a greater reactance. In ideal DC steady state, the inductor acts as a short; this does not mean current changes instantaneously during switching.\nWith current as the sine reference, p(t) = UI sin(2ωt). Positive intervals store energy; negative intervals return it. The mean power of an ideal inductor is zero.\n$$P_L=0,\\qquad Q_L=UI=I^2X_L=\\frac{U^2}{X_L}$$\nReactive power is measured in var. The RMS relation I = U/(ωL) is valid, but i = u/(ωL) is not: the instantaneous signals are shifted by 90°. Similarly U/I = ωL is a real magnitude ratio, whereas U̲/I̲ = jωL is a complex phasor ratio. Mixing these forms loses phase information.","experiment":"inductor"},{"id":"capacitor-ac","title":"A capacitor: current leads voltage","sources":[{"deck":"ac-basics","page":61},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":62},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":63},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":64},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":65},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":66},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":67},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":68},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":69},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":70},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":71},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":72}],"body":"A capacitor stores energy in its electric field. Current changes its charge and therefore its voltage:\n$$i=C\\frac{du}{dt},\\qquad X_C=\\frac1{\\omega C},\\qquad Z_C=\\frac1{j\\omega C}=-jX_C$$\nCurrent leads voltage by 90°. The RMS relation is U = XC I; the corresponding phasor equation is:\n$$\\underline U=-jX_C\\underline I$$\nIncreasing frequency or capacitance decreases reactance. In ideal DC steady state, a capacitor is an open circuit. Its voltage cannot change instantaneously with finite current.\nAn ideal capacitor alternately stores and returns energy, with zero average active power. With the voltage as a sine reference, its instantaneous power is UI sin(2ωt). The sign of reactive power is negative:\n$$P_C=0,\\qquad Q_C=-UI=-I^2X_C=-\\frac{U^2}{X_C}$$\nUse a common phase reference when comparing capacitor and inductor power waveforms. For C = 1 μF and u(t) = 70.7√2 sin(314t − π/6) V, XC = 1/(314·10⁻⁶) ≈ 3184.71 Ω and I = 70.7/XC ≈ 22.1998 mA. Thus I̲ ≈ 22.2∠60° mA and i(t) ≈ 22.2√2 sin(314t + π/3) mA. The lecture rounds XC to 3180 Ω.\nWith current instead of voltage as the sine reference, pC(t) = −UI sin(2ωt). This is the opposite of the inductor power waveform under that same current reference.","experiment":"capacitor"},{"id":"rlc-summary","title":"Compare R, L and C","sources":[{"deck":"ac-basics","page":73},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":74},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":75},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":76},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":77},{"deck":"ac-basics","page":78}],"body":"For associated voltage and current references, the three impedance relations share one form: U̲ = ZI̲. Resistance has real impedance R and consumes active power. Inductance has positive imaginary impedance jωL and positive reactive power. Capacitance has negative imaginary impedance −j/(ωC) and negative reactive power.\nFor R, voltage and current are in phase. For L, voltage leads current by 90°. For C, current leads voltage by 90°. Their average powers are I²R, zero and zero respectively for ideal elements.\nUse the comparison table to keep the time-domain relation, phasor impedance, phase and power together before combining elements."}]},{"id":"ac-networks","title":"AC networks, power and compensation","subtitle":"Lesson06 · AC-2","outcomes":["Reduce series and parallel impedance networks.","Solve AC circuits by node equations and equivalents.","Calculate power and improve power factor without changing the load voltage."],"topics":[{"id":"rl-series","title":"An RL series circuit and three triangles","sources":[{"deck":"ac2","page":2},{"deck":"ac2","page":3},{"deck":"ac2","page":4},{"deck":"ac2","page":5},{"deck":"ac2","page":6},{"deck":"ac2","page":7},{"deck":"ac2","page":8},{"deck":"ac2","page":9},{"deck":"ac2","page":10},{"deck":"ac2","page":11},{"deck":"ac2","page":12},{"deck":"ac2","page":13}],"body":"A series resistor and inductor carry the same current. Their voltages add instantaneously and as phasors; their RMS magnitudes do not add arithmetically.\n$$u=Ri+L\\frac{di}{dt},\\qquad Z=R+jX_L,\\qquad\\underline U=Z\\underline I$$\n$$|Z|=\\sqrt{R^2+X_L^2},\\qquad\\varphi=\\tan^{-1}\\frac{X_L}{R},\\qquad U=I|Z|$$\nVoltage leads current by φ. The resistor voltage is in phase with current; the inductor voltage is 90° ahead. Thus U² = UR² + UL².\nMultiplying the voltage triangle by I gives the power triangle: P = URI, Q = ULI and S = UI. The impedance, voltage and power triangles have the same angle φ.\n$$S^2=P^2+Q^2,\\qquad\\cos\\varphi=\\frac PS$$"},{"id":"rlc-series","title":"A series RLC circuit","sources":[{"deck":"ac2","page":14},{"deck":"ac2","page":15},{"deck":"ac2","page":16},{"deck":"ac2","page":17},{"deck":"ac2","page":18},{"deck":"ac2","page":19},{"deck":"ac2","page":20}],"body":"For series R, L and C, add the instantaneous component voltages or their phasors. With the steady-state sinusoidal solution:\n$$Z=R+j(X_L-X_C),\\qquad\\underline U=\\underline U_R+\\underline U_L+\\underline U_C=Z\\underline I$$\n$$|Z|=\\sqrt{R^2+(X_L-X_C)^2},\\qquad\\varphi=\\operatorname{atan2}(X_L-X_C,R)$$\nIf XL > XC, the circuit is inductive and current lags voltage. If XL < XC, it is capacitive and current leads. If XL = XC, the input is resistive and voltage and current are in phase.\nThe vertical sides of the voltage and power triangles are differences: UL − UC and QL + QC, because QC is negative. Individual reactive voltages can exceed the supply magnitude while opposing each other in the phasor sum.","experiment":"series","check":{"question":"Must current always lag in a series RLC circuit?","options":["Yes","No; it depends on XL − XC"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Current lags when XL > XC, leads when XL < XC, and is in phase when they are equal."},"sections":[{"title":"Time-domain voltage equation","body":"The capacitor voltage includes its initial value:\n$$u(t)=Ri(t)+L\\frac{di}{dt}+u_C(t_0)+\\frac1C\\int_{t_0}^t i(\\tau)\\,d\\tau$$\nThe phasor relation describes the sinusoidal steady-state part at one frequency. Initial-condition transients are not represented by that single-frequency phasor."}]},{"id":"impedance-networks","title":"Series and parallel impedances","sources":[{"deck":"ac2","page":21},{"deck":"ac2","page":22},{"deck":"ac2","page":23},{"deck":"ac2","page":24},{"deck":"ac2","page":25},{"deck":"ac2","page":26}],"body":"Combine impedances using the same network rules as resistances, but keep the quantities complex throughout.\n$$Z_{\\rm series}=Z_1+Z_2,\\qquad Z_{\\rm parallel}=\\frac{Z_1Z_2}{Z_1+Z_2}$$\nVoltage division and current division are phasor equations:\n$$\\underline U_2=\\frac{Z_2}{Z_1+Z_2}\\underline U,\\qquad\\underline I_1=\\frac{Z_2}{Z_1+Z_2}\\underline I$$\nWith U̲ = 100∠60° V, Z₁ = j20 Ω, Z₂ = 10 − j10 Ω and ω = 1000 rad/s, voltage division gives U̲₂ = 100∠−30° V. Therefore u₂(t) = 100√2 sin(1000t − 30°) V.\nFor positive series resistors alone, a component voltage cannot exceed the supply RMS voltage. In a series RLC circuit it can. Find a general passive two-terminal network’s impedance from a suitable test phasor: Z = U̲/I̲.","check":{"question":"Can a component’s RMS voltage exceed the supply RMS voltage in a series RLC circuit?","options":["Yes","No"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Inductive and capacitive voltages oppose each other. Their phasor sum can be smaller than either individual magnitude."}},{"id":"ac-analysis","title":"Solve the phasor circuit, then return to time","sources":[{"deck":"ac2","page":27},{"deck":"ac2","page":28},{"deck":"ac2","page":29},{"deck":"ac2","page":30},{"deck":"ac2","page":31},{"deck":"ac2","page":32},{"deck":"ac2","page":33}],"body":"Draw the phasor model without changing its topology. Replace sources by same-frequency RMS phasors and elements by R, jωL or 1/(jωC). Write KCL, KVL, node equations or an equivalent-source relation, solve in complex numbers, then express the requested answer as a magnitude/phase or a time function.\nIn the ammeter/voltmeter example, choose U̲BC = 100∠0° V. The branch currents are I̲₁ = j10 A and I̲₂ = 10 − j10 A, so the ammeter reads |I̲| = 10 A. Adding U̲AB = −j100 V gives U̲AC = 100 − j100 V; the voltmeter reads 100√2 V, approximately 141 V.\nFor the meter example, the A–B series capacitor has impedance −j10 Ω. Between B and C are two parallel branches: a capacitor of impedance −j10 Ω and a series branch with impedance 5 + j5 Ω. The ammeter is in the total-current path and the voltmeter measures A–C. Both instruments read RMS magnitudes.","sections":[{"title":"A general network by nodes and by Thévenin","body":"Let A be the upper node and B ground. Four parallel branches connect them: R₁ then a voltage source U̲ with its positive terminal at B; R₂ + jXL; R₃ − jXC; and R₄ in series with a current source I̲ directed upward. Define i₁, i₂ and i₃ downward. The source fixes the fourth branch current, irrespective of R₄.\n$$\\underline V_A=\\frac{\\underline I-\\underline U/R_1}{1/R_1+1/(R_2+jX_L)+1/(R_3-jX_C)}$$\n$$\\underline i_1=\\frac{\\underline V_A+\\underline U}{R_1},\\quad\\underline i_2=\\frac{\\underline V_A}{R_2+jX_L},\\quad\\underline i_3=\\frac{\\underline V_A}{R_3-jX_C}$$\nAlternatively remove the current-source/R₄ branch and find the equivalent across A–B. Set Zp = (R₂ + jXL)∥(R₃ − jXC). Then U̲AB,oc = −U̲Zp/(R₁ + Zp) and ZAB = R₁∥(R₂ + jXL)∥(R₃ − jXC). The voltage across the upward current source, top relative to bottom, is U̲source = U̲AB,oc + I̲(ZAB + R₄)."}]},{"id":"ac-power","title":"Active, reactive and apparent power","sources":[{"deck":"ac2","page":34},{"deck":"ac2","page":35}],"body":"Use RMS magnitudes and let φ = φu − φi. With current entering the positive voltage terminal:\n$$P=UI\\cos\\varphi,\\qquad Q=UI\\sin\\varphi,\\qquad S=UI=\\sqrt{P^2+Q^2}$$\nP is in watts, Q in var and S in volt-amperes. Positive P means absorbed average power; negative P means supplied average power. Positive Q is inductive, and negative Q is capacitive. The phasor product is complex power, U̲I̲* = P + jQ; its magnitude is S.\nThe active-network example uses uₛ(t) = 50√2 sin(1000t) V and iₛ(t) = 10√2 sin(1000t + 90°) A. The indicated part has P = −250 W, Q = 250 var and S = 250√2 VA. It supplies 250 W of average power under the stated absorbed-power reference.","experiment":"power","warning":"The AC-2 material describes P < 0 as “power consumption,” including the −250 W example. With current entering the positive voltage terminal, P < 0 means power is supplied by that part of the circuit. The numerical result is retained; the interpretation is corrected.","sections":[{"title":"Reconstruct the active-network example","body":"At ω = 1000 rad/s, L = 10 mH gives jXL = j10 Ω and C = 200 μF gives −jXC = −j5 Ω. A 50∠0° V source feeds node A through L. From A to ground are R₁ = 5 Ω in series with C, and R₂ = 2 Ω in series with an upward 10∠90° A current source. The selected two-terminal part contains L and both right-hand branches; the 50 V source is outside it.\n$$\\underline V_A=\\frac{50/(j10)+j10}{1/(j10)+1/(5-j5)}=j50\\ \\mathrm V$$\n$$\\underline I=\\frac{50-j50}{j10}=-5-j5=5\\sqrt2\\angle(-135^\\circ)\\ \\mathrm A$$\nI̲ enters the selected part’s positive 50 V terminal. Its absorbed complex power is 50(−5 + j5) = −250 + j250 VA. Therefore P = −250 W, Q = +250 var and S = 250√2 VA: the selected part supplies average power while absorbing positive reactive power."}]},{"id":"power-factor","title":"Why power factor changes line current","sources":[{"deck":"ac2","page":36},{"deck":"ac2","page":37},{"deck":"ac2","page":38}],"body":"For a fixed RMS supply voltage and fixed active load power:\n$$I=\\frac{P}{U\\cos\\varphi}$$\nA smaller power factor requires larger line current and therefore more I²R loss in the supply wiring. A pure resistor has power factor 1; ideal pure L or C has power factor 0. The lecture contrasts lightly loaded motors, loaded motors and fluorescent lamps as examples of different inductive load power factors.\nAt 220 V, a 40 W load with unity power factor draws about 0.182 A. At power factor 0.5, a 40 W load draws about 0.364 A. Equal active power does not imply equal current or equal apparent power."},{"id":"compensation","title":"Parallel-capacitor compensation","sources":[{"deck":"ac2","page":39},{"deck":"ac2","page":40},{"deck":"ac2","page":41},{"deck":"ac2","page":42},{"deck":"ac2","page":43},{"deck":"ac2","page":44},{"deck":"ac2","page":45}],"body":"Keep the original load’s RMS voltage and active power unchanged. A parallel capacitor supplies leading reactive current locally, reducing the lagging reactive component drawn from the source. It does not change the original load current when its voltage is fixed.\nIf an inductive load has initial phase angle φL and the desired input angle is φ:\n$$C=\\frac{P}{\\omega U^2}(\\tan\\varphi_L-\\tan\\varphi)$$\nFor P = 40 W, U = 220 V, f = 50 Hz and initial power factor 0.5, correction to unity requires about 4.56 μF. Line current falls from about 0.364 A to 0.182 A. The original slide rounds the capacitance to 4.5 μF.\nUndercompensation leaves a lagging input. Exact compensation gives an in-phase input. Overcompensation gives a leading input and again increases current relative to unity power factor.\nA series capacitor can change the input power factor, but it also changes the original load voltage and operating condition. It does not satisfy this compensation problem’s requirement to leave the load voltage unchanged.","check":{"question":"Why does a series capacitor not solve the stated load-preserving compensation problem?","options":["It cannot change input power factor","It changes the original load voltage"],"answer":1,"explanation":"It can change input power factor, but it changes the voltage across the original load. The task requires that load voltage and active power to remain unchanged."}}]},{"id":"periodic-signals","title":"Non-sinusoidal periodic circuits","subtitle":"Lesson06 · Harmonics supplement","outcomes":["Separate a periodic waveform into DC and harmonic components.","Solve each frequency with its own impedance.","Calculate RMS, average power and total power factor."],"topics":[{"id":"periodic-waveforms","title":"Where non-sinusoidal signals arise","sources":[{"deck":"ac2","page":47},{"deck":"ac2","page":48},{"deck":"ac2","page":49},{"deck":"ac2","page":50},{"deck":"ac2","page":51}],"body":"A circuit can have a non-sinusoidal input, or a nonlinear element can generate harmonics even when its input is sinusoidal. The lecture uses a transistor circuit, a half-wave rectifier and an analog oscilloscope’s sawtooth scan as examples.\nOther periodic waveforms include a digital clock pulse train, a triangle wave, a sawtooth and a full-wave-rectified waveform. They are periodic but cannot each be represented by one sinusoidal phasor."},{"id":"fourier","title":"Decompose first, solve one frequency at a time","sources":[{"deck":"ac2","page":52},{"deck":"ac2","page":53},{"deck":"ac2","page":54},{"deck":"ac2","page":55},{"deck":"ac2","page":56}],"body":"A periodic function satisfying the stated Dirichlet conditions—absolute integrability over a period, finitely many extrema and finitely many ordinary discontinuities—can be expanded in a Fourier series. The series has a DC term, a fundamental and harmonics at integer multiples of the fundamental frequency.\n$$f(t)=B_0+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}B_{nm}\\sin(n\\omega_0t+\\psi_n),\\qquad\\omega_0=\\frac{2\\pi}{T}$$\nFor a 50% duty-cycle pulse between 0 and Im, the DC term is Im/2 and the odd-harmonic amplitudes are 2Im/(nπ), with the time origin chosen for the illustrated pulse.\nIn a linear circuit, calculate the DC response and each harmonic response separately. Recalculate XL = nωL and XC = 1/(nωC) for each harmonic. If the sources are already expressed as distinct sinusoids, no Fourier decomposition is needed.\nConvert each solved phasor back to its time-domain function before adding different frequencies. You may add instantaneous responses of different frequencies; you may not add their phasors as one same-frequency vector sum.","warning":"The pulse-wave Fourier coefficient in the supplied AC-2 material is garbled/inconsistent. For the drawn 0-to-Im, 50% duty pulse, integrating the Fourier coefficients gives DC = Im/2 and odd sine coefficients 2Im/(nπ), not Im/(2π)."},{"id":"harmonic-power","title":"RMS and power across harmonics","sources":[{"deck":"ac2","page":57},{"deck":"ac2","page":58},{"deck":"ac2","page":59},{"deck":"ac2","page":60}],"body":"Over a full fundamental period, different harmonics are orthogonal. With Uk and Ik denoting harmonic RMS values:\n$$U=\\sqrt{U_0^2+\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty}U_k^2},\\qquad I=\\sqrt{I_0^2+\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty}I_k^2}$$\n$$P=U_0I_0+\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty}U_kI_k\\cos\\varphi_k$$\nOnly voltage and current components at the same frequency contribute to their average-power product. The lecture also introduces the signed harmonic sum Q = Σ UkIk sin φk as a definition of reactive power for this treatment.\nFor non-sinusoidal signals, total power factor is P/(UI). It is not generally the cosine of one physical phase difference, and the sinusoidal identity S² = P² + Q² does not generally hold with that harmonic-sum definition of Q."},{"id":"harmonic-example","title":"A circuit that removes one harmonic","sources":[{"deck":"ac2","page":61},{"deck":"ac2","page":62},{"deck":"ac2","page":63},{"deck":"ac2","page":64}],"body":"The source is u(t) = 60[1 + √2 sin(ωt) + √2 sin(2ωt)] V. At the fundamental, 1/(ωC₁) = 400 Ω, ωL₁ = 100 Ω, and 1/(ωC₂) = ωL₂ = 100 Ω; R = 100 Ω. Find the voltage across C₂ using the exact network shown below.\nFor DC, inductors are shorts and capacitors are opens, giving UC₂,DC = 60 V. At the fundamental, the L₂–C₂ series branch resonates. The L₁∥C₁ impedance is j400/3 Ω, giving I̲₂ = 0.45∠−90° A and U̲C₂ = 45∠−180° V.\nAt twice the fundamental frequency, L₁∥C₁ is at parallel resonance and blocks that component. Adding the time-domain results:\n$$u_{C2}(t)=60-45\\sqrt2\\sin(\\omega t)\\ \\mathrm V$$"},{"id":"harmonic-rms-example","title":"A numerical RMS and power-factor example","sources":[{"deck":"ac2","page":65},{"deck":"ac2","page":66}],"body":"Use the voltage and current exactly as given:\n$$u=10+3\\sqrt2\\sin\\omega t+0.3\\sqrt2\\sin3\\omega t+0.2\\sqrt2\\sin5\\omega t$$\n$$i=0.2+0.1\\sqrt2\\sin\\omega t+0.5\\sqrt2\\sin5\\omega t$$\nThere is no third-harmonic current, so the third-harmonic voltage contributes no average power. The DC, fundamental and fifth-harmonic contributions give P = 10(0.2) + 3(0.1) + 0.2(0.5) = 2.4 W.\n$$U=\\sqrt{109.13}\\approx10.447\\ \\mathrm V,\\quad I=\\sqrt{0.3}\\approx0.5477\\ \\mathrm A$$\nUsing unrounded values gives total power factor approximately 0.4195. Rounding intermediate current and voltage explains the small difference in the displayed example."}]},{"id":"resonance-filters","title":"Resonance, filters and frequency response","subtitle":"AC (3) · Resonance and transfer functions","outcomes":["Find series and parallel resonance conditions.","Relate quality factor, bandwidth and selectivity.","Read transfer functions, filter responses and Bode plots."],"topics":[{"id":"series-resonance","title":"Series resonance","sources":[{"deck":"resonance","page":3},{"deck":"resonance","page":4},{"deck":"resonance","page":5},{"deck":"resonance","page":6},{"deck":"resonance","page":7},{"deck":"resonance","page":8},{"deck":"resonance","page":9}],"body":"A circuit containing inductance and capacitance is at input resonance when its input voltage and current are in phase. In the series RLC circuit, this happens when the inductive and capacitive reactances cancel.\n$$\\omega_0L=\\frac1{\\omega_0C},\\qquad\\omega_0=\\frac1{\\sqrt{LC}},\\qquad f_0=\\frac1{2\\pi\\sqrt{LC}}$$\nAt resonance Z = Rtotal and I₀ = U/Rtotal. The current is maximal for fixed source voltage and fixed series resistance. Inductor and capacitor voltages oppose each other and can each be larger than the total source voltage. The voltage across the total series resistance equals the source voltage; an external resistor alone receives only its share if the coil also has winding resistance.\nA circuit containing only positive R and L, or only positive R and C, does not exhibit this finite-frequency LC cancellation. With ideal zero resistance, series resonance has zero input impedance; the usual finite-current formula needs a nonzero total resistance.","check":{"question":"Can positive R and L alone produce finite-frequency LC series resonance?","options":["Yes","No"],"answer":1,"explanation":"There is no capacitive reactance to cancel the inductive reactance. The series-resonance condition needs both L and C."}},{"id":"quality-bandwidth","title":"Quality factor, bandwidth and selectivity","sources":[{"deck":"resonance","page":10},{"deck":"resonance","page":11},{"deck":"resonance","page":12},{"deck":"resonance","page":13},{"deck":"resonance","page":20}],"body":"At series resonance, quality factor is the ratio of the inductor or capacitor voltage magnitude to the source voltage:\n$$Q=\\frac{\\omega_0L}{R_{\\rm total}}=\\frac1{\\omega_0CR_{\\rm total}}=\\frac1{R_{\\rm total}}\\sqrt{\\frac LC}$$\nThe cutoff frequencies are the half-power points, where I = I₀/√2. Their difference is the bandwidth:\n$$BW=f_2-f_1=\\frac{f_0}{Q}=\\frac{R_{\\rm total}}{2\\pi L}$$\nHigher Q gives a narrower resonance curve and greater selectivity. Increasing total resistance lowers the peak current and broadens the curve without changing the ideal series resonant frequency. Decreasing L or C increases f₀; with fixed U and resistance the resonant current remains U/Rtotal.","check":{"question":"For the same resonant frequency, which Q gives narrower bandwidth?","options":["A higher Q","A lower Q"],"answer":0,"explanation":"BW = f0/Q, so a higher Q gives a narrower passband and greater selectivity."}},{"id":"resonance-worked","title":"Include the coil resistance","sources":[{"deck":"resonance","page":14},{"deck":"resonance","page":15}],"body":"The example has external R = 10 Ω, coil resistance r = 10 Ω, L = 100 mH, C = 2 μF and U = 3 V RMS. The total series resistance is 20 Ω, not 10 Ω.\n$$f_0\\approx355.88\\ \\mathrm{Hz},\\quad I_0=0.15\\ \\mathrm A,\\quad X_L=X_C\\approx223.607\\ \\Omega$$\n$$Q\\approx11.180,\\quad U_R=1.5\\ \\mathrm V,\\quad U_L=U_C\\approx33.541\\ \\mathrm V$$\nThe coil terminal voltage includes its winding resistance and ideal inductance: |Ur + jUL| ≈ 33.575 V. The bandwidth is approximately 31.831 Hz. Distinguish the voltage of the ideal L from the terminal voltage of the real coil."},{"id":"tuning","title":"Using resonance to select a station","sources":[{"deck":"resonance","page":16},{"deck":"resonance","page":17},{"deck":"resonance","page":18},{"deck":"resonance","page":19}],"body":"The antenna picks up signals at several frequencies. An adjustable capacitor with the input coil changes the resonant frequency and selects one signal. A coupled coil transfers the selected signal to the transistor amplifier.\n$$C=\\frac1{(2\\pi f_0)^2L}$$\nThe tuning example has L₁ = 250 μH, winding resistance 20 Ω and desired station frequency f₁ = 820 kHz. The formula gives C ≈ 150.685 pF, rounded to 150 pF in the lecture. The selected signal then passes through the coupled winding L₂ to the transistor amplifier. In the receiver example, this input tuning precedes mixing/frequency conversion, intermediate-frequency amplification, detection and audio amplification; the six-transistor schematic illustrates these later stages rather than changing the input resonance calculation."},{"id":"parallel-resonance","title":"Ideal and lossy parallel resonance","sources":[{"deck":"resonance","page":21},{"deck":"resonance","page":22},{"deck":"resonance","page":23},{"deck":"resonance","page":24},{"deck":"resonance","page":25},{"deck":"resonance","page":26}],"body":"For ideal L and C in parallel, their admittances cancel at ω₀ = 1/√LC. The input admittance is zero and the ideal input impedance is infinite. The inductor and capacitor branch currents can be large while their input-current sum is zero.\nFor a real coil with series resistance r and inductance L in parallel with C, set the imaginary part of the input admittance to zero:\n$$Y=\\frac1{r+j\\omega L}+j\\omega C$$\n$$\\omega_0=\\sqrt{\\frac1{LC}-\\frac{r^2}{L^2}},\\qquad Z_0=\\frac L{rC}$$\nThis finite positive resonance requires r² < L/C. The resonant input impedance is real. At high Q the resonant frequency is close to 1/√LC and the branch currents may greatly exceed the total current; this is why the lecture calls it current resonance.","check":{"question":"At their ideal lossless resonance, a series LC and a parallel LC behave respectively as…","options":["a short and an open","an open and a short"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The series impedances cancel; the parallel admittances cancel. These are ideal limiting models."},"sections":[{"title":"Resonance versus the impedance maximum","body":"For the lossy coil, zero input phase and maximum impedance magnitude are not exactly the same frequency. The lecture’s identification of the two is a high-Q approximation. At the zero-phase frequency the impedance is exactly L/(rC); the precise peak of |Z| is slightly shifted when coil loss is appreciable. For r = 0, ideal parallel resonance gives an open input and ideal series resonance gives a short input, subject to the usual steady-state idealizations."}],"warning":"The resonance slides identify the zero-phase frequency with the maximum-impedance frequency for a lossy coil in parallel with C. These coincide only approximately at high Q. The zero-phase formulas shown here are exact for the stated series-r coil model."},{"id":"mixed-resonance","title":"More than one resonance in one network","sources":[{"deck":"resonance","page":27}],"body":"The supplementary network combines an L₁∥C₂ parallel branch with another capacitor C₃ in series. At the parallel branch’s resonance, its impedance has a pole. Below that frequency the parallel branch is inductive; above it the branch is capacitive.\nAt a lower frequency, the inductive behaviour of that branch can cancel the series capacitor, producing a series resonance. Track the sign of the input reactance on each side of its zero and its pole; the two resonances have different input behaviour.","sections":[{"title":"Locate the zero and pole","body":"$$Z=j\\left(\\frac{\\omega L_1}{1-\\omega^2L_1C_2}-\\frac1{\\omega C_3}\\right)$$\n$$\\omega_{\\rm series}=\\frac1{\\sqrt{L_1(C_2+C_3)}},\\qquad\\omega_{\\rm parallel}=\\frac1{\\sqrt{L_1C_2}}$$\nWith positive component values, the series zero lies below the parallel pole. Below the zero the input is capacitive; between zero and pole it is inductive; above the pole it is capacitive again."}]},{"id":"transfer-functions","title":"Describe response as a function of frequency","sources":[{"deck":"resonance","page":28},{"deck":"resonance","page":29}],"body":"A frequency response describes how a network’s voltage or current response changes as the frequency of a sinusoidal input changes. Define the input, output and their reference directions before taking a ratio.\n$$H(j\\omega)=\\frac{\\underline U_{\\rm out}}{\\underline U_{\\rm in}}=|H(j\\omega)|e^{j\\arg H(j\\omega)}$$\nThe magnitude is the amplitude-frequency characteristic. The angle is the phase-frequency characteristic. A transfer function contains both; magnitude alone cannot reconstruct the output waveform’s timing."},{"id":"low-high-pass","title":"First-order low-pass and high-pass filters","sources":[{"deck":"resonance","page":30},{"deck":"resonance","page":31},{"deck":"resonance","page":32},{"deck":"resonance","page":33},{"deck":"resonance","page":34}],"body":"For the RC low-pass circuit, take the output across C. Low frequencies pass and high frequencies are attenuated:\n$$H_{LP}=\\frac1{1+j\\omega RC},\\quad |H_{LP}|=\\frac1{\\sqrt{1+(\\omega RC)^2}},\\quad\\arg H_{LP}=-\\tan^{-1}(\\omega RC)$$\nFor the RC high-pass circuit, take the output across R. High frequencies pass and low frequencies are attenuated:\n$$H_{HP}=\\frac{j\\omega RC}{1+j\\omega RC},\\quad |H_{HP}|=\\frac{\\omega RC}{\\sqrt{1+(\\omega RC)^2}}$$\nThe high-pass phase is 90° − arctan(ωRC) for positive frequency. Both circuits have cutoff ωc = 1/(RC). At cutoff, magnitude is 1/√2 of the passband value and phase is −45° for the low-pass or +45° for the high-pass."},{"id":"filter-bode","title":"Filter types, decibels and Bode plots","sources":[{"deck":"resonance","page":35},{"deck":"resonance","page":36},{"deck":"resonance","page":37},{"deck":"resonance","page":38}],"body":"Filters can be passive or active, and first-order or higher-order. Their passband shapes may be low-pass, high-pass, band-pass or band-stop. Ideal rectangular response curves are useful targets; physical filters have transitions. The examples include a band-pass combination and a twin-T band-stop network.\nA Bode magnitude plot uses logarithmic frequency on the horizontal axis and decibels on the vertical axis. For a voltage transfer ratio:\n$$G_{\\rm dB}=20\\log_{10}|H|$$\nFor a power ratio, use 10 log₁₀(P₂/P₁). The voltage-ratio expression corresponds to that power-ratio expression when the compared impedances are equal.\nA first-order RC low-pass is approximately flat below cutoff and falls at 20 dB per decade above it. Its exact cutoff magnitude is about −3.01 dB, not the 0 dB intersection of the straight-line asymptotes. Read the phase response alongside the magnitude.","sections":[{"title":"Band-pass and twin-T examples","body":"A high-pass stage followed by a low-pass stage can pass an intermediate band when the high-pass cutoff is lower than the low-pass cutoff. If stages load each other, derive the combined transfer function from their connected circuit instead of multiplying unloaded divider responses.\nThe twin-T band-stop topology connects two T networks in parallel between input and output. One T uses two series resistors with a capacitor from their midpoint to ground. The other uses two series capacitors with a resistor from their midpoint to ground. Their paths cancel at the notch under the appropriate component-ratio condition. The lecture’s figure introduces this topology as a band-stop example; it does not assign numerical component values."}]},{"id":"frequency-examples","title":"Phase shifting and frequency selection","sources":[{"deck":"resonance","page":39},{"deck":"resonance","page":40},{"deck":"resonance","page":41},{"deck":"resonance","page":42},{"deck":"resonance","page":43},{"deck":"resonance","page":44}],"body":"The phase-shifting bridge example varies R₁ while keeping the output RMS magnitude constant. Follow the shown voltage-divider phasors: the output phase sweeps from 180° at one endpoint toward 0° at the other. The diagram fixes the input and output polarities, so changing those references would also change the reported angle.\nThe frequency-selection example combines L, C₁ and C₂. First require the L∥C₁ branch to resonate at 3ω, blocking that component. Then choose C₂ so the series reactive impedance vanishes at ω, leaving the load resistance R. These are two different equations imposed at two different frequencies; do not use one reactance value for both.\nFor the frequency-selection circuit, L = 0.12 H and ω = 314 rad/s. The input is ui = √2U₁ sin ωt + √2U₂ sin 3ωt and the desired output across R is uo = √2U₁ sin ωt.","sections":[{"title":"Phase-shifting bridge derivation","body":"The left divider is R over R; the right divider is R₁ over C. Define input top rail relative to bottom rail and output left midpoint relative to right midpoint. With the output unloaded, the left midpoint is Ui/2 and the right midpoint is Ui/(1 + jωR₁C). Thus:\n$$\\frac{\\underline U_o}{\\underline U_i}=\\frac{j\\omega R_1C-1}{2(1+j\\omega R_1C)}$$\n$$|U_o|=|U_i|/2,\\qquad\\arg(U_o/U_i)=180^\\circ-2\\tan^{-1}(\\omega R_1C)$$\nFor Ui = 2U∠0°, the output RMS value remains U as R₁ changes from zero toward infinity, while its phase moves from 180° toward 0°. Loading the output would change this divider result."},{"title":"Reject the third harmonic","body":"Require 3ωL = 1/(3ωC₁), giving:\n$$C_1=\\frac1{9\\omega^2L}\\approx9.3909\\ \\mu\\mathrm F$$\nAt ω, the parallel branch has impedance jωL/(1 − ω²LC₁) = j(9/8)ωL. Cancel it with C₂:\n$$\\frac1{\\omega C_2}=\\frac98\\omega L,\\qquad C_2=\\frac8{9\\omega^2L}\\approx75.127\\ \\mu\\mathrm F$$\nAt the fundamental, the reactive series combination is a short, so all input voltage appears across R. At the third harmonic, L∥C₁ is an open, so no third-harmonic load current flows. The complete circuit impedance at the fundamental is R, not zero."}]}]},{"id":"lab-instruments","title":"Basic electrical instruments","subtitle":"Experiment 1 · Measurement and circuit laws","outcomes":["Identify instrument terminals and controls.","Measure voltage and current with the shown reference connections.","Compare measured circuits with Kirchhoff and Thévenin predictions."],"topics":[{"id":"instrument-controls","title":"Multimeter, oscilloscope and generator","sources":[{"deck":"lab1","page":4},{"deck":"lab1","page":5},{"deck":"lab1","page":6},{"deck":"lab1","page":7},{"deck":"lab1","page":8},{"deck":"lab1","page":9},{"deck":"lab1","page":10},{"deck":"lab1","page":11},{"deck":"lab1","page":12},{"deck":"lab1","page":13},{"deck":"lab1","page":14}],"body":"The instruments are a digital multimeter, an oscilloscope and a function generator. The multimeter shown has a common terminal, a voltage/resistance/continuity/diode/capacitance terminal, a μA/mA current terminal marked up to 400 mA, and a separate current terminal marked up to 10 A. These ratings describe the lecture’s instrument; check the markings on the instrument in use.\nFor a stable AC waveform, the oscilloscope’s AUTO function gives an initial display. Select the channel, adjust vertical amplitude and position, horizontal time scale and position, and trigger settings. Connect the probe tip to the signal and its ground to circuit common ground. For an upper resistor whose terminals are both above common ground, a probe ground clipped to one of its terminals changes the circuit. Use the multimeter for that floating voltage as instructed in the laboratory.\nThe lecture’s two-channel function generator offers frequency, amplitude and phase settings. Its stated maximum frequency is 60 MHz and maximum output amplitude is 20 Vpp. Use the signal-path table and control guide below to distinguish signal generation, voltage measurement and waveform acquisition.","sections":[{"title":"What happens inside each instrument","body":"A digital multimeter routes DC through a DC attenuator, AC through an AC attenuator and AC converter, and resistance through an ohms converter. A current input uses a shunt to produce a voltage. The selected signal reaches the analog-to-digital converter (ADC), which compares against a precision reference. Its digital result drives the display and can also reach a BCD output or interface.\nThe oscilloscope vertical path contains an attenuator, amplifier and filter before the ADC. Acquisition processing works with memory and post-processing before the display. A parallel trigger path takes the conditioned input through a trigger system to the horizontal time-base system, which coordinates acquisition. Volts/div changes vertical scale; time/div changes the horizontal scale; position moves the trace; trigger settings stabilize repeated acquisitions.\nThe generator diagram uses direct digital synthesis (DDS): a standard clock drives an address-computing unit, which addresses waveform memory. Waveform data reaches a DAC, filtering and output conditioning. In the illustrated implementation, the clock is 11.728 MHz, the address-computing unit is 48-bit, memory uses 13 address bits and stores 12-bit words in 8K locations, and the waveform-output DAC is 12-bit. A comparator provides rectangular-wave generation; amplitude-adjustment DACs, attenuation, a 20 dB amplifier and an offset DAC set the output. The shown attenuator spans 0 to −58 dB in 2 dB steps, with 1 V / 10 V ranges. Trigger/gate input and channel sync outputs coordinate signals. These internal example specifications are distinct from the front-panel 60 MHz / 20 Vpp ratings of the laboratory generator."}]},{"id":"board","title":"The experiment board","sources":[{"deck":"lab1","page":15}],"body":"Identify the +5 V and +2.5 V supplies, 1 mA current-source terminals, variable resistor, ground and USB power connection before wiring a circuit. Use the printed terminal labels on the actual board to locate each connection. All common-ground terminals are electrically connected; the low side of the DC supply is grounded. The second laboratory warns that the current-source arrow printed on its board is opposite to its current-flow direction. Check that board-specific convention before using a signed current in the equations."},{"id":"measure-dc","title":"Voltage, current and Kirchhoff measurements","sources":[{"deck":"lab1","page":16},{"deck":"lab1","page":18}],"body":"For the series-parallel circuit, calculate theoretical values first. Use a 5 V source through R₁ = 1 kΩ feeding R₂ = 4.7 kΩ in parallel with R₃ = 2 kΩ; all three lower terminals share ground. Build this circuit, measure V₁ and V₂ with the oscilloscope, and measure I₁, I₂ and I₃ separately with the digital multimeter. Compare measurements with the prediction using the same directions and polarities.\nFor the Kirchhoff circuit, connect a 5 V node through R₁ = 2 kΩ and a 2.5 V node through R₂ = 4.7 kΩ to the shared node V₃, with R₃ = 10 kΩ from V₃ to ground. Calculate the voltages and currents of R₁, R₂ and R₃ using KCL and KVL, then measure them. Use signed values when evaluating the node and loop balances rather than adding all magnitudes.","sections":[{"title":"Theoretical predictions","body":"For the series-parallel circuit, R₂∥R₃ ≈ 1.402985 kΩ, so V₁ = 5 V and V₂ ≈ 2.9193 V. Currents are I₁ ≈ 2.0807 mA, I₂ ≈ 0.6211 mA and I₃ ≈ 1.4596 mA. Check I₁ = I₂ + I₃ and 5 = I₁R₁ + V₂.\nFor the two-supply circuit, define I₁ from 5 V toward V₃, I₂ from 2.5 V toward V₃ and I₃ down to ground.\n$$V_3=\\frac{5/2+2.5/4.7}{1/2+1/4.7+1/10}\\ \\mathrm V$$\nCalculate I₁ = (5 − V₃)/2, I₂ = (2.5 − V₃)/4.7 and I₃ = V₃/10 in mA. A negative I₂ means its physical current returns toward the 2.5 V supply. Record theoretical and measured signed values side by side.\nNumerically V₃ ≈ 3.7304 V, I₁ ≈ 0.6348 mA, I₂ ≈ -0.2618 mA and I₃ ≈ 0.3730 mA."}]},{"id":"measure-thevenin","title":"Measure load power and the maximum-power point","sources":[{"deck":"lab1","page":17}],"body":"A 5 V source feeds a junction through 1 kΩ. At the junction, a 4.7 kΩ resistor goes to ground and a 1 mA current source injects upward. A 2 kΩ resistor connects the junction to the top of RL; RL returns to ground. Vary RL from 500 Ω to 10 kΩ and record ten measurement points. Measure VL with the oscilloscope and IL with the multimeter. Calculate PL = VLIL for each point and plot PL against RL.\nCompare the measured maximum-power point with the Thévenin prediction. Explain differences between theoretical and measured values, including the component and instrument behaviour relevant to the measurements. Disconnect the connecting wires when measuring RL as a resistance with the multimeter.","sections":[{"title":"Predict the power curve","body":"Remove RL. No current flows in the 2 kΩ resistor, so Uth = (5/1 kΩ + 1 mA)/(1/1 kΩ + 1/4.7 kΩ) ≈ 4.94737 V. With independent sources deactivated, Rth = 2 kΩ + (1 kΩ∥4.7 kΩ) ≈ 2.82456 kΩ. The predicted maximum therefore occurs near RL = 2.82456 kΩ, with PL,max ≈ 2.16639 mW. Compare the measured ten-point curve with this prediction using the actual resistor values."}]},{"id":"measure-phase","title":"Combine two sinusoidal sources through resistors","sources":[{"deck":"lab1","page":19}],"body":"The waveform experiment uses two 50 Hz sources v₁ and v₂ with peak-to-peak voltages 10 V and 5 V. Connect v₁ through 2 kΩ and v₂ through 4.7 kΩ to the shared node v₃, with 10 kΩ from v₃ to common ground. Set v₁ as the 0° reference; set v₂ to 0°, 60°, 120° and 180°. Measure all three node voltages with the oscilloscope.\nThis circuit produces a resistor-weighted sum, not v₁ + v₂ directly. Its RMS phasor equation is:\n$$\\underline V_3=\\frac{\\underline V_1/(2\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega})+\\underline V_2/(4.7\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega})}{1/(2\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega})+1/(4.7\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega})+1/(10\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega})}$$\nUse V̲₁ = 5/√2∠0° V and V̲₂ = 2.5/√2∠θ V. For any sine wave, Vpp = 2Vm = 2√2V. Compare measured amplitudes and phase with the weighted phasor prediction, and keep the oscilloscope grounds at common ground."},{"id":"instrument-report","title":"Record the experiment clearly","sources":[{"deck":"lab1","page":3},{"deck":"lab1","page":20}],"body":"Keep the objectives, theoretical preparation and original data tables. Obtain the teaching assistant’s signature on the original data as required by the slide. The experiment report includes the preparation, data processing, summary and reflection. Submit the completed report through the course learning platform."}]},{"id":"lab-resonance","title":"Measure a series RLC frequency response","subtitle":"Experiment 2 · Resonance curves","outcomes":["Measure resonant frequency and the current–frequency curve.","Compare changed resistance and capacitance.","Use measurements to estimate inductance and coil resistance."],"topics":[{"id":"resonance-instruments","title":"Equipment and connections","sources":[{"deck":"lab2","page":2},{"deck":"lab2","page":3},{"deck":"lab2","page":4},{"deck":"lab2","page":5},{"deck":"lab2","page":6},{"deck":"lab2","page":7},{"deck":"lab2","page":8},{"deck":"lab2","page":9}],"body":"Use the circuit board, function generator, multimeter and oscilloscope. All board ground terminals are connected together, and the low side of the DC supply is grounded. The slide notes that the pictured current-source arrow is opposite to the board’s current-flow direction; preserve that board-specific note when interpreting measurements.\nOn the generator, select a channel and waveform, set frequency, amplitude and phase, then enable its output. Select the multimeter function and correct terminal before measurement; current measurement uses a series connection. On the oscilloscope, Measure gives RMS and peak-to-peak readings. Acquire → XY enables the Lissajous display; its line or ellipse helps compare the phases of the input and resistor voltage.\nConnect probe grounds to circuit common ground. In XY mode, a rising straight line indicates in-phase signals; a falling straight line indicates 180° opposition. Intermediate phase differences generally give an ellipse. Equal normalized amplitudes and a 90° shift give a circle."},{"id":"resonance-setup","title":"Set up the initial RLC circuit","sources":[{"deck":"lab2","page":10},{"deck":"lab2","page":11},{"deck":"lab2","page":12}],"body":"Use R = 10 Ω, nominal C = 1 μF and L = 100 mH. Measure the actual capacitance and the coil resistance. Connect C, the real coil (L in series with its winding resistance r), and R in series, with R next to the common-ground return. Connect the generator across the whole chain. Put channel 1 at the input and channel 2 at the top of R; both probe ground clips go to the common-ground return.\nCalculate the expected resonant frequency using the measured component values. Set the generator near that frequency with approximately 2 V RMS across the RLC circuit. Display the total voltage U on channel 1 and UR on channel 2; UR is in phase with current.\nIn XY mode, fine-tune frequency until u and uR are in phase. Record UC, UL, the voltage across the capacitor–coil combination, and UR; compare them with the theoretical values. Distinguish the physical coil’s terminal voltage from the ideal inductance voltage when accounting for winding resistance."},{"id":"frequency-sweep","title":"Measure the 27-point resonance curve","sources":[{"deck":"lab2","page":13}],"body":"Keep the measured circuit voltage U constant at 2 V RMS as frequency changes. Obtain current from I = UR/R. Use the resonant frequency and the following offsets on both sides:\n$$0,\\quad\\pm5,\\pm10,\\pm15,\\pm20,\\pm25,\\pm35,\\pm45,\\pm55,\\pm65,\\pm85,\\pm105,\\pm155,\\pm205\\ \\mathrm{Hz}$$\nThis gives 27 points: five 5 Hz steps, four 10 Hz steps, two 20 Hz steps and two 50 Hz steps on each side. Observe how the phase between U and UR changes across resonance; UR has the same phase as current."},{"id":"change-components","title":"Repeat with different R and C","sources":[{"deck":"lab2","page":14}],"body":"For part II, replace the external resistor with R = 30 Ω and repeat part I. Keep the other parameters unchanged.\nFor part III, use C = 0.5 μF with the other parameters returned to part I. Measure the resonant frequency, current and capacitor and coil voltages at resonance. Keep the circuit voltage approximately 2 V RMS.\nCompare changes in resonant frequency, peak current, Q and bandwidth. Include coil resistance in total series resistance when predicting the peak and bandwidth."},{"id":"resonance-report","title":"Analyse the measured curves","sources":[{"deck":"lab2","page":15},{"deck":"lab2","page":16}],"body":"Plot the current–frequency curves for parts I and II on the same axes. Compare their peak frequencies, peak currents and widths. Use the measured capacitance and resonant frequency to calculate inductance.\nExplain the expected voltage across the capacitor–coil combination at resonance and the measured residual. Estimate coil resistance from the resonant measurements and compare it with the measured DC resistance; a practical AC coil need not have the same effective loss resistance at the experiment frequency.\nUse the data to explain how R, L and C affect f₀, Q and bandwidth. Attach the pre-lab work, original data, processed tables and curves, answers to the analysis questions, and a summary of observations and causes of discrepancies."}]}];
